    values_to = "Value",
    cols = c(contains("RMSE"),contains("R-Squared"))
  ) %>% 
  mutate(Metric = ifelse(str_detect(Model,"RMSE"),"RMSE","R-Squared"),
         Sample = ifelse(Sample == "E","Train","Test"),
         Mod = case_when(
           str_detect(Model,"Set 1") ~ "Set 1",
           str_detect(Model,"Set 2") ~ "Set 2",
           str_detect(Model,"Set 3") ~ "Set 3",
         )) %>% select(-Model) %>% 
  pivot_wider(names_from = "Metric",values_from = "Value") %>%
  datasummary_df(fmt =2,
               title = "Summary of Prediction Performance Across Models",
               align = "lccccccc", output = "tinytable")
```
Overall, the models do a reasonable job at predicting birthweight. The training birthweight SD is 585.80 and the testing is 595.08, which are very similar. For the in-sample OLS, the first feature predicts the birthweight about 100grams better than the SD in both the train and test sample. As we add more features, we get minor gains in predictive performance, but this is only about 30 grams for the most "overfit" model. Across models, the in-sample R-squared is slightly higher. 

Table 1 describes the in and out-of-sample goodness of fit statistics for each of the three feature sets used.
In the first model, the in-sample RMSE approximates the out-of-sample RMSE quite well. The difference is within about 10 units. In the second model, the difference is also similar. In the third model, we see the same pattern. Each model's in-sample fit understates the RMSE out-of-sample RMSE by 10 or so points. 

A key lesson from this exercise is that conventional OLS methods tend to overstate the predictive performance of models on in-sample data. This is because OLS is minimizing with respect to the sample. It is therefore necessary to evaluate machine learning tools on testing samples to understand how they fit data they have never seen. In general, as we add predictors, we can enrich our predictions, but this may result in overfitting which is problematic for maing good predictions. 

